     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg a day.\n- The usual maximum single dose is 800 mg, so 1600 mg is double that.\n- The usual maximum daily dose in adults is 3,200 mg, so 6,400 mg is double that too.\n- Doses this high carry a serious risk of stomach bleeding, sudden kidney injury, heart problems and other toxicity, even in a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThis looks like it could be a transcription error. For example, the intended order might have been 400 mg four times daily (1,600 mg a day), or 800 mg three times daily.\n\n**2. A licensed prescriber needs to place the order.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders must come from a licensed prescriber with privileges here, such as the patient's attending physician, resident, NP or PA. That keeps the order traceable to the person who has clinical responsibility for it. This is standard regulatory and patient-safety practice, and it applies no matter who in the hospital is asking.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Please ask the patient's treating team on the medicine service to check what dose they meant.\n- Once they confirm, they can enter the order themselves or ask me to enter it. I'm happy to help them with that.\n- If you'd like, I can draft a short message to the team flagging the dose question."
      }
     ],
