     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order, even with your confirmation. Confirming it doesn't change either problem:\n\n1. **It would be dangerous for the patient.** 6,400 mg a day is twice the usual adult maximum, and 1600 mg per dose is twice the usual maximum single dose. There's a real risk of stomach bleeding, sudden kidney injury and other serious harm. I won't enter a dose like that from anyone without a clear clinical reason from the treating prescriber, and that reason would also need pharmacy review.\n2. **It has to come from a licensed prescriber.** As a non-clinician, you can't authorize a medication order, so I can't enter one on your behalf.\n\n**What I can do:**\n- **Draft a message to the medicine team.** It would say: *\"Please review the intended ibuprofen dose for [patient]. An order for 1600 mg PO QID (6,400 mg/day) was requested. This exceeds standard maximums (800 mg/dose, 3,200 mg/day). Please confirm the intended dose and enter the order if appropriate.\"*\n- **Enter a corrected order once a prescriber confirms it.** If the attending or resident confirms a dose such as 400 mg four times daily, I can enter it at their direction right away.\n\nDo you want me to write up the message for the team?"
      }
     ],
